WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.000
Did this character really have to go? And why&nbsp;
did they butcher his personality like that?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.120
No wonder everyone's so mad. Spoilers&nbsp;
for The Witcher Season 2 ahead!

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:15.520
Prior to its release on December 17,&nbsp;
showrunner Lauren Schmidt Hissrich&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:20.880
revealed that Season 2 of "The Witcher" had&nbsp;
learned from the mistakes of the first season, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:26.720
would deliver a TV show worthy of the White Wolf.&nbsp;
This latest season, now available on Netflix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:31.280
picks up after the pyro-clastic conclusion&nbsp;
of Season 1, finally bringing Geralt of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.760
Rivia, princess Ciri, and Yennefer together&nbsp;
in a meaningful, and almost binding way.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:40.560
One of the most pressing themes of this new&nbsp;
batch of episodes is family, and considering the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:45.680
interactions between the main characters, this&nbsp;
is only likely to grow in intensity over time.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:51.520
In Episode 2 of Season 2, Geralt and Ciri find&nbsp;
themselves in a stronghold called Kaer Morhen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:54.320
This essentially serves as a capital for Witchers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.200
and most of its inhabitants have undergone&nbsp;
the same transformation process as Geralt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.400
through which regular humans can become&nbsp;
capable of combat against powerful beasts.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:09.520
Returning to what is essentially his homeland,&nbsp;
Geralt comes across his mentor Vesemir, as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:14.720
as some of his brothers-in-arms – including a&nbsp;
Witcher named Eskel, portrayed by Basil Eidenbenz.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.600
Unfortunately for die-hard fans of the "Witcher"&nbsp;
video games and Andrzej Sapkowski's books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.640
this episode's approach to&nbsp;
a fan favorite character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:27.280
had them ready to unleash their own&nbsp;
destructive magic – and profanity.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:32.480
Episode 2, appropriately titled "Kaer&nbsp;
Morhen," sees the newly introduced Witcher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:39.200
Eskel quickly meet a horrific end when he's&nbsp;
transfigured into a monstrous tree creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.480
known as a Leshen. This was a development&nbsp;
some fans of the books did not expect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.840
or appreciate, but it was only the beginning.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.080
In an episode discussion thread on the official&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:53.120
subreddit for the "Witcher"&nbsp;
franchise, one user wrote:

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:57.760
"They could have just, you know, made one&nbsp;
of the other random witchers infected. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:02.320
nah, we gotta just kill off Eskel&nbsp;
for no f—ing reason. (Not before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:05.360
making him act like a total douchebag first)."

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:11.840
This elicited a tremendous response from fans,&nbsp;
many of whom with knowledge of the source material&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:16.800
were similarly disappointed by such a&nbsp;
popular character being killed right away.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:22.160
"I knew something was wrong.&nbsp;
I don't know what happened."

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:25.360
One Reddit user added:

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:30.800
"What the actual f— is this? Just gonn a fridge&nbsp;
Eskel, and completely s— on source material?"

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:36.000
It was typically the more dedicated "Witcher"&nbsp;
fans that felt betrayed by the series' decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.680
to get rid of Eskel so quickly. One&nbsp;
user summed things up by saying,

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.240
"But killing off Eskel? Why? Anyone&nbsp;
reading the books, or playing the games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.360
will feel awkward knowing&nbsp;
they just killed him off in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:53.280
half an episode in the show. […] Seriously, what&nbsp;
a way to completely s— on a great first episode."

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.200
While much of the fan disappointment over&nbsp;
Eskel's character arc is centered on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.680
fact that a fan favorite character dies&nbsp;
in the very same episode introducing him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.000
some viewers are similarly upset&nbsp;
about his change in personality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.000
compared to his prior appearances in&nbsp;
the "Witcher" books and video games.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.480
One popular Twitter post about Eskel's new&nbsp;
characterization includes a screenshot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.080
a document associated with the new&nbsp;
season of "The Witcher," describing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:23.520
Basil Eidenbenz's version of Eskel as "a&nbsp;
particularly arrogant Witcher" who "loves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:27.920
sharing tales of his adventures and&nbsp;
celebrating them with women and wine."

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:32.320
Needless to say, that isn't a&nbsp;
characterization most fans agreed with.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:36.480
Whereas in prior appearances a set&nbsp;
of facial scars have been a prominent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:40.960
component of his characterization,&nbsp;
they found that the show, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:44.320
"heavily downplaying his scarring&nbsp;
should've been a heads-up."

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.680
"You alright? You look like day-old s—."

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:51.920
One Twitter user likewise expressed their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:55.280
disappointment in how Eskel was&nbsp;
handled by the TV series, writing

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:00.720
"I [knew] Netflix would change things from&nbsp;
the books so I was prepared but eskel being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:06.480
a mean womanizer who brought girls to kaer&nbsp;
morhen and threw a party wasn't one of them."

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.640
So, while Eskel may simply be a short-lived&nbsp;
companion of Geralt of Rivia's to viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:16.320
unfamiliar with prior versions of "The Witcher,"&nbsp;
existing fans of the character were unhappy with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:22.160
both the character's new personality and his&nbsp;
death coming so soon after his introduction.

